  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\nAspirin\n\nChief Complaint:\nRLQ abdominal and R flank pain\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\n___ y.o. man, ___ only speaking, transferred from OSH where \nhe presented from the nursing home with 2 weeks of abdominal \npain which had worsened over the last 2 days.  Patient had been \ncomplaining of crampy abdominal pain for the past few weeks. He \ndescribes the pain as sharp, intermittent, starting in the \nmid-epigastric area and then migrating to the lower abd \nquadrants. Initially, the pain was worse with eating and he did \nnot have any pain unassociated with meals. However, in the past \n___ days, the pain has become constant. It is 10+/10 in \nintensity at its worst; pain meds help control the pain. In \naddition to the pain, pt reports nausea and multiple episodes of \nnon-bilious/non-bloody emesis. He denies diarrhea; has been \nconstipated at home, using Mg citrate with 1BM/day. Denies BRBPR \nor tarry stools. Presented to OSH where CT abdomen was done \nwithout IV contrast due to creatinine of 1.8. CT was poor \nquality and inconclusive. Given the colicky pain, patient was \ntransferred here for concern for mesenteric ischemia. Of note, \npatient has h/o afib but coumadin discontinued for ? \ncoumadin-induced skin necrosis.  \n\nIn the ED, initial vs= T99.3, HR 97, BP 127/69, RR 16, 98%ra.  \nHis Cr was 1.8 (baseline 1.4). Patient received 1.5L fluids, \ngiven morphine for pain. Surgery evaluated the patient; not \nlikely mesenteric ischemia at this time, but will follow. CT \nwith IV contrast delayed given rise in Cr.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\n___ M with PMH notable for afib, CRF with Cr 1.4, HTN and \nGERD, transferred from OSH for ? mesenteric ischemia, with \nrising Cr, lactate and LDH, found to have R renal infarct.\n\n1) Abdominal pain: Patient presented to ___ with \npostprandial midepigastric pain with radiation to the RLQ and R \nflank of 2 weeks duration with exacerbation over the last ___ \ndays. A contrast abd CT was not performed due to chronic kidney \ninsufficiency with Cr 1.4. Patient was transferred to ___ for \n? mesenteric ischemia. In the ED, he was afebrile with normal \nvital signs. His Cr was found to be increased to 1.8 from 1.4 \nearlier that same day at the OSH. Patient received 1.5L fluids \nand morphine for pain. Surgery consult was obtained, who thought \nit was unlikely his symptoms were consistent with acute \nmesenteric ischemia. Patient was admitted to medicine for workup \nof acute on chronic kidney failure. Upon arrival to the floor, \npatient had 10+ out 10 crampy midepigastric pain with radiation \nto the RLQ and R flank. He was afebrile but nauseaous. He was \nstarted on heparin drip for Atrial fibrillation with possible \nembolism to GI tract or other.  We put him on continuous IVF for \nhydration and maintained him NPO in the event that his kidney \nfunction improved by the next morning and he received the \ncontrast abd CT. Repeat labs on the evening of admission \nrevealed Cr 1.9. Labs from the next morning revealed Cr 2.1, \nlactate 2.4, WBC 18.4 (from 7.8 on admission) and LDH 1483. LTFs \nwere also mildly elevated (Alt 98, Ast 129, normal AP and \ntBili). On exam, patient had severe R CVA tenderness; his \nabdominal exam and symptoms had improved. per surgery team, \nacute mesenteric ischemia was even more unlikely in this \nsetting. Based on the exam findings, renal consult was obtained \nto evaluate for ? R renal infarct. Doppler renal US showed low \nperfusion to the R kidney, but was otherwise inconclusive. \nNon-contrast abd CT was also inconclusive as it did not show any \nprimary evidence for renal infarct. Per renal consult, thorough \nworkup for glomerulonephritis, pyelonephritis and vasculitis \nsuch as HSP. Results showed normal C3, C4, IgM, IgG, IgA, \nnegative HbsAg, HbsAb, HbcAb, HCV-AB and ANCA. Urine analysis \nwas revealing of a few RBC casts, no muddy brown casts were \nnoted. A radioactive renal scan was obtained on HD#4, which \nconfirmed decreased tracer uptake in the midportion of the right \nkidney consistent with R renal infarction. Patient kidney \nfunction gradually improved, with Cr 1.7 at time of discharge. \nPrimary care physician, ___, was notified about \npatient's diagnosis, and after discussion with her, we decided \nto start patient on lovenox 80mg daily (renally-dosed) x7 days \nand coumadin 5mg Qhs. Patient was discharged home after \nreceiving instructions on how to self-inject lovenox. He seemed \nto understand the risks of non-complying with the above \nanticoagulants and will follow with Dr. ___ frequent INR \nchecks. His first appt with Dr. ___ is on ___.\n\n2)Acute on chronic kidney insufficiency: Please see above. \nPatient's Cr increased despite hydration with IVF. Given \nsuspicion for renal infarct, a renal doppler US and non-contrast \nabd CT were intially obtained, both of which were inconclusive. \nRenal scan confirmed R right renal infarct. This was likely due \nto renal thromboemboli in the setting of atrial fibrillation \nwithout anticoagulation. Patient Cr started trending downward on \nHD#4, suggesting that the renal infarct was not expanding. \nPatient needs to be followed by nephrology as an outpatient. Dr. \n___ was notified.\n\n3) Hypoxia: During this hospitalization, the patient was found \non a couple occasions satting as low as mid ___ on RA while \nlying flat in bed. He was completely asymptomatic during these \nepisodes; however, did state that he liked supplemental oxygen \nfor comfort. CXR was obtained which showed L basilar atelectasis \nand minimal L pleural effusion, likely due to volume overload. \nHe received 40mg IV lasix once with 2 liter diuresis.  No frank \npulmonary edema was noted on the CXR, however. O2 sat improved \nremarkably reaching the low ___ after patient was encouraged to \ntake deep breaths. He also maintained his O2sat ~94% RA with \nambulation, suggesting that the low sats were mostly due to \nshallow breathing and partly from volume overload. His O2 sats \nimproved with incentive spirometry. \n\n4) Fevers: During this hospitalization, patient ran fevers of \nhigh 90 to low 100s, with highest T 101.3. Blood cultures were \nobtained and are negative to date. Tylenol was given PRN fever.  \nHe defervesced.\n\n5) HTN: Pt was maintained on home BP meds with good control.  \n\n6) constipation: maintained on bowel regimen consisting of senna \nand colace.  \n\n7) FEN: repleted lytes PRN. Patient tolerated PO well by time of \ndischarge.\n\n8) Ppx: Initially placed on heparin gtt and then transitioned to \nlovenox upon discharge. Omeprazole for ppx heartburn.\n\n9) Code: presumed full\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nPrimary diagnoses:\n1) Bilateral renal infarct, R>L\n2) Possible chronic mesenteric ischemia\n\nSecondary diagnoses:\n1) atrial fibrillation\n2) HTN\n3) Chronic kidney disease",
